    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a house in oregon. gotta use a big douglas fir beam for the main span. the span is gonna be about 14 feet. i'm worried about some of the knots and potential internal check/splits in the timber i'm looking at. what should i be looking out for in terms of load bearing capacity if the wood isn't perfectly clear?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When assessing the load-bearing capacity of a lumber with knots or internal checks, you should consider the characteristics of the knot or check itself rather than the direction of the grain. Knots with a relatively smooth face and no severe taper or occlusion can still have sufficient strength. Internal checks or splits can significantly weaken the lumber, so avoid beams with cracks, rough faces, or signs of active decay. Typically, a beefed-up beam or additional nogs or brackets can help support the load around a weakened section, but consult a structural engineer or experienced woodworker for specific guidance. Monitoring the beam's condition during construction and installation is also crucial.",
      "source": "target",
